; Desktop Subsystem for Android (DSA)
; The guest's sensors HAL (/vendor/bin/hw/dsa-sensors) runs in the platform's sensors HAL
; domain, labelled by vendor_file_contexts, which already lets it serve
; android.hardware.sensors.ISensors/default and write the framework's event queue. It reads the
; host's sensors over vsock, from the host's sensor port; this rule lets it open that
; connection.

(allow hal_sensors_server self (vsock_socket (create connect read write getattr setopt getopt shutdown)))

; The framework's sensor service, in system_server, makes the HAL's event queue and its wake
; lock queue as memfds (system_server_tmpfs) and hands them over in ISensors.initialize. The
; platform's policy expects them on /dev/ashmem and has no rule for system_server's memfds,
; so binder refused the call that carried them on every boot ("avc: denied { read write }
; ... scontext=u:r:hal_sensors_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:system_server_tmpfs:s0"), the
; HAL never got a queue to write events into and no app ever saw a sensor change. A direct
; channel an app opens is a memfd of the app's (appdomain_tmpfs), which the platform lets the
; HAL use only through Android 17's memfd_file class.
(allow hal_sensors_server system_server_tmpfs (file (read write getattr map)))
(allow hal_sensors_server appdomain_tmpfs (file (read write getattr map)))
